  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "C.D.Shaijan vs Food Inspector on 22 February, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Monday, The 22Nd Day Of February 2016/3Rd Phalguna, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1190 of 2004 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 61/2002 of I Addl.Sessions Court, Ernakulam Cc 716/1998 of J.M.F.C.-I, Kochi Revision Petitioner(S)/Appellant/Accused: --------------------------------------- C.D.Shaijan, Chelattu House, Neericode P.O., Alwaye. By Adv. Sri.Sheji P.Abraham Respondent(S)/Respondent/Complainant: ------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Kochi Circle, Kochi. 2. State Of Kerala, Rep. By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam By Public Prosecutor Sri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 22-02-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. C.D.Shaijan vs Food Inspector on 22 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/22378891/ 1 B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P.No. 1190 of 2004 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 22nd day of February, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 30.6.1998 at about 3.30 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector purchased one litre of cow milk from the first accused. The second accused was the nominee of A3 company, which manufactured and marketed the milk. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. Ext . P17 report of the public analyst would show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for the food item and was therefore adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext. P19 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would also show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for cow milk and was therefore adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW3 were examined and Exts.P1 to P28 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 and DW2 were examined for the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P19 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P19 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) C.D.Shaijan vs Food Inspector on 22 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/22378891/ 2 that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P19 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P19 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) C.D.Shaijan vs Food Inspector on 22 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/22378891/ 2 that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
